      <ArticleTitle>Early lactate clearance and length of stay in a synthetic intensive care cohort.</ArticleTitle>
      <Abstract>
        <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Raised blood lactate is a marker of tissue hypoperfusion in critically ill adults. Whether early clearance relates to the duration of intensive care is uncertain.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We followed adults admitted to a mixed intensive care unit and measured lactate at admission and six hours later. Clearance was the relative fall between the two values.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients whose lactate fell by less than ten percent stayed longer in the unit and needed vasopressors more often. The association remained after adjustment for illness severity (p &lt; 0.05).</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Poor early lactate clearance is associated with a longer intensive care unit length of stay.</AbstractText>
