      <ArticleTitle>Rising serum creatinine in the first day of <i>intensive care</i> and the risk of acute kidney injury.</ArticleTitle>
      <Abstract>
        <AbstractText>Serum creatinine was recorded every six hours in adults during their first day of intensive care. A rise of 0.3 mg/dL or more within 48 hours defined acute kidney injury, as in the KDIGO criteria. Patients with an early rise received renal replacement therapy more often and remained in the unit for longer. Urine output below 0.5 mL/kg/h for six hours added information to creatinine alone. Monitoring both markers early may identify patients at risk of a prolonged stay; values were expressed per 1.73 m<sup>2</sup> where relevant.</AbstractText>
      </Abstract>
